# Erdős Problem #99 — transverse blocker matching on the outer shadow

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** Target C-II, near-low-`t` canonical full-core branch  
**Claim class:** proof-only geometric refinement of the outer-shadow theorem  
**External input:** none

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The exact outer-shadow identity pays the angular misalignment of every outer-walk contact edge against its corresponding loaded-hull side. If we retain only edge occurrences lying in a narrow cone around that side, an internal retained occurrence has two nearly antipodal contact rays.

For cone half-width

\[
0<\alpha<\pi/12,
\]

triangle-freeness makes these near-antipodal contact pairs a **matching** in the contact-neighbor set of each source vertex. This removes the factor-five occurrence loss from the coarse blocker theorem.

Let `G_alpha` be the number of internal outer-walk occurrences whose incoming and outgoing oriented unit edges both make angle `<alpha` with the corresponding loaded hull-side direction. Let `E_tr(alpha)` be the number of distinct selected blocker contact edges after one blocker is chosen at every source vertex supporting such an occurrence, except loaded degree-two vertices.

Then finitely

\[
\boxed{
2s_B+4E_{tr}(\alpha)
\ge
x_B-{2(b-P_F)\over1-\cos\alpha}.
}
\tag{TM.1}

Moreover every selected blocker edge can be associated with one loaded hull side so that its absolute transverse component relative to that side is strictly greater than

\[
\boxed{
Y_\alpha:=\sin(\pi/3-\alpha).
}
\tag{TM.2}

Thus the low-`t` outer shadow cannot be supported only by almost-tangent contacts.

For the explicit safe choice

\[
\alpha=\pi/13<\pi/12,
\]

and the AG58 Section-171 endpoint,

\[
\boxed{
{2s_B+4E_{tr}(\pi/13)\over D}
\ge1.12538696889038\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{TM.3}

while every selected blocker has transverse component

\[
\boxed{
>\sin(10\pi/39)
=0.721202447343814\ldots .
}
\tag{TM.4}

Consequently at least one of `s_B/D` or `E_tr/D` has limsup at least

\[
\boxed{0.187564494815064\ldots .}
\tag{TM.5}

The weighted inequality (TM.3), not the max corollary, is the main statement.

---

## 1. Angular projection budget

Retain the loaded-hull interval notation from the outer-shadow theorem. On interval `W_i`, let `nu_i` be the unit chord direction and `e` an oriented unit outer-walk edge occurrence. Exact vector summation gives

\[
 b-P_F
 =\sum_{i,e}(1-\nu_i\cdot e).
\tag{1.1}

Call an edge occurrence `alpha`-bad if its unsigned oriented angle from `nu_i` is at least `alpha`. Then

\[
1-\nu_i\cdot e\ge1-\cos\alpha.
\]

Therefore the total bad-edge occurrence count satisfies

\[
\boxed{
N_{bad}(\alpha)
\le{b-P_F\over1-\cos\alpha}.
}
\tag{1.2}

There are exactly `x_B=b-q` internal positions on the loaded-hull outer-walk intervals. One bad edge occurrence spoils at most two such positions, so

\[
\boxed{
G_\alpha
\ge x_B-{2(b-P_F)\over1-\cos\alpha}.
}
\tag{1.3}

---

## 2. Every good occurrence is a near-antipodal contact pair

At an `alpha`-good internal occurrence, let the incoming and outgoing oriented walk-edge vectors be `e_-` and `e_+`. Both lie within angle `alpha` of `nu_i`.

The two corresponding contact rays from the source vertex are

\[
-e_-,\qquad e_+.
\]

Their angular separation from exact antipodality is at most `2alpha`; equivalently their smaller mutual angle is at least

\[
\pi-2\alpha.
\tag{2.1}

Call any pair of contact rays satisfying (2.1) an `alpha`-antipodal pair.

---

## 3. For `alpha<pi/12`, the antipodal pairs form a matching

Fix one contact ray `r`. Any second ray `r'` forming an `alpha`-antipodal pair with it must lie within angular distance `2alpha` of the antipodal ray `-r`. Thus all possible partners of `r` lie in one angular arc of total width

\[
4\alpha<\pi/3.
\]

Triangle-free unit separation makes any two distinct contact-neighbor rays at one source vertex separated by strictly more than `pi/3`. Therefore the partner arc contains at most one contact ray.

Hence:

\[
\boxed{
\text{the graph of `alpha`-antipodal contact-ray pairs at one vertex is a matching.}
}
\tag{3.1}

Positive-core contact degree is at most five, so there are at most two such unordered pairs at any source vertex.

For degree at least three, one unordered consecutive pair of incident edges determines at most one face-sector occurrence: the complementary sector contains at least one other incident ray. Thus a degree-`>=3` source vertex supports at most two `alpha`-good outer-face occurrences.

A loaded degree-two vertex has only one ray pair but the same outer face may occupy both sectors, so it supports at most two occurrences as well.

---

## 4. Degree-two versus blocker classification

An `alpha`-good occurrence uses two distinct physical contact incidences. A degree-one bridge turnaround would traverse one edge in opposite directions, and the two oriented occurrences could not both lie within `alpha<pi/2` of the same `nu_i`.

If its source vertex has contact degree two and is unloaded, positive contact equilibrium would require an exact straight balanced two-contact chain. Canonical C26/C35 excludes unloaded positive-core degree-two vertices in C-II. Therefore every degree-two source vertex supporting a good occurrence is loaded and contributes one unit to

\[
s_B=\sum_{b\in B}(3-\deg_C b).
\]

Every other supporting vertex has contact degree at least three. Choose one contact edge at that vertex not belonging to one selected good pair and call it a blocker.

Let `V_tr` be the number of distinct blocker vertices. Section 3 gives

\[
G_\alpha\le2s_B+2V_{tr}.
\tag{4.1}

One physical blocker edge can be selected at at most its two endpoints, so

\[
V_{tr}\le2E_{tr}(\alpha).
\tag{4.2}

Combining (1.3), (4.1), and (4.2) proves (TM.1).

---

## 5. Every blocker is strongly transverse

Fix a blocker vertex and the selected `alpha`-good occurrence used to define it. Let `u=nu_i` and let `n` be a unit normal.

The two walk contact rays lie within `alpha` of `+u` and `-u`. Every third contact ray is separated from each of them by strictly more than `pi/3`.

If the projective angle of the blocker from the axis `Ru` were at most `pi/3-alpha`, it would lie within angular distance at most

\[
(\pi/3-\alpha)+\alpha=\pi/3
\]

of one of the two walk contact rays, contradiction.

Therefore its projective angle from `u` is greater than `pi/3-alpha`, and

\[
\boxed{
|n\cdot e_{block}|>\sin(\pi/3-\alpha)=Y_\alpha.
}
\tag{5.1}

No sign is asserted: for a deep outer indentation the blocker may point toward or away from the loaded hull side. The theorem is an absolute transverse-penetration statement.

---

## 6. AG58 endpoint arithmetic

At the safe AG58 Section-171 endpoint put

\[
t=t_{58}=1.820922037979444\ldots,
\]

\[
\delta={b-P_F\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D}.
\]

The feasible low-`t` budget is

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R_{58}=0.00575825.
\tag{6.1}

Also

\[
{x_B\over D}=\pi+\delta-t-\kappa+o(1).
\]

For fixed `alpha`, (TM.1) has normalized right side

\[
\pi-t-\kappa
+\delta\left(1-{2\over1-\cos\alpha}\right).
\tag{6.2}

Use the largest feasible `kappa=2R58-4delta`. This becomes

\[
\pi-t-2R_{58}
+\delta\left(5-{2\over1-\cos\alpha}\right).
\tag{6.3}

For every `alpha<pi/12`, the coefficient of `delta` is negative, so the worst case is

\[
\delta=R_{58}/2,
\qquad
\kappa=0.
\]

With `alpha=pi/13`, outward numerical evaluation gives

\[
\boxed{
\pi-t+{R_{58}\over2}
-{R_{58}\over1-\cos(\pi/13)}
=1.12538696889038\ldots .
}
\tag{6.4}

Also

\[
Y_{\pi/13}
=\sin\left({\pi\over3}-{\pi\over13}\right)
=\sin(10\pi/39)
=0.721202447343814\ldots .
\]

This proves (TM.3)--(TM.4). If both `s_B/D` and `E_tr/D` were smaller than one sixth of the right side of (TM.3), then `2s_B+4E_tr` would be too small. Hence (TM.5).

---

## 7. Frontier effect

Near the current `q-k` floor the actual source boundary must therefore exhibit one of two first-order mechanisms:

```text
loaded-slack branch:
    >=0.18756 D loaded degree slack;

transverse-network branch:
    >=0.18756 D distinct blocker contacts;
    each can be tagged to a loaded hull side with transverse component >0.7212.
```

The branch constants are deliberately symmetric and weaker than the weighted tradeoff. If `s_B` is independently small, (TM.3) forces nearly `0.28D` transverse blocker edges.

This theorem subsumes the local transverse geometry behind shallow two-edge teeth and extends it to arbitrary near-geodesic loaded-hull shadow routes, including the rounded long-side branch.

---

## 8. Scope and remaining obstruction

- The theorem is occurrence-safe in the presence of bridges and repeated articulation vertices.
- The matching argument uses only the strict `>pi/3` contact-ray separation and `alpha<pi/12`.
- The transverse component is absolute; no inward sign is claimed for a general outer-shadow blocker.
- `E_tr` counts physical unit-contact edges, not KKT weight and not stopped trains.
- Contact-isolated source points do not occur on the positive-contact outer walk.
- No strict sub-`D` competitor or exact unit triangle is obtained.

The next target is global: couple this linear transverse contact population to the train-phase system or show that the loaded-slack branch is incompatible with the near-extremal diameter thrackle.

**Disposition:** proof-only geometric strengthening. C-II remains open.
